WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.413 --> 00:00:17.338
that his lantern might cause the barrel of gunpowder to explode, the Persian tossed it aside.

2
00:00:18.259 --> 00:00:21.502
It broke, leaving the Persian and Raoul in the dark.

3
00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:28.268
They couldn't even rely on the light shining through the open trapdoor because it had been switched off.

4
00:00:29.088 --> 00:00:32.530
"We must return to the hexagon room and try to speak with Christine."

5
00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:35.331
The Persian stumbled against a barrel.

6
00:00:36.011 --> 00:00:40.413
"With our discovery of the gunpowder, I finally understand Erik's threat.

7
00:00:41.154 --> 00:00:48.117
If Christine doesn't agree to marry him, he'll blow up the opera house at eleven o'clock tomorrow night!"

8
00:00:48.998 --> 00:00:50.298
Raoul was stunned.

9
00:00:50.318 --> 00:00:53.760
"At that hour the opera house will be full of people!

10
00:00:54.212 --> 00:00:58.876
But Christine will never agree to spend the rest of her life with such a monster."

11
00:00:58.896 --> 00:01:07.042
"I don't think she realizes the life-or-death consequences of her decision," the Persian said as they carefully climbed

12
00:01:07.103 --> 00:01:08.944
the stairs to the hexagon room.

13
00:01:08.964 --> 00:01:13.067
"Eleven o'clock tomorrow night."

14
00:01:13.588 --> 00:01:18.091
Raoul repeated as the two men sat on the floor in total darkness.

15
00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:21.654
"I wonder what time it is," the Persian said.

16
00:01:21.674 --> 00:01:23.536
"My watch has stopped."

17
00:01:24.552 --> 00:01:31.176
"It seems like we've been stuck in this mirrored prison for years," Raoul put his watch up to

18
00:01:31.216 --> 00:01:31.576
his ear.

19
00:01:31.596 --> 00:01:36.599
"Mine is still ticking, but I can't see the hands to tell the time."

20
00:01:36.619 --> 00:01:38.860
"Break the glass and touch the hands."

21
00:01:39.741 --> 00:01:41.242
Raoul did as he was told.

22
00:01:41.262 --> 00:01:47.725
"Judging by the space between the hands, I'd say it's almost 11 o'clock."

23
00:01:47.745 --> 00:01:50.087
"But is that morning or the evening?"

24
00:01:50.927 --> 00:01:51.708
"I don't know!

25
00:01:52.236 --> 00:01:54.697
Who could keep track of time when we were in the forest?

26
00:01:54.998 --> 00:01:55.858
Or in the desert?"

27
00:01:56.719 --> 00:02:00.421
Raoul's voice grew louder as he became more frustrated.

28
00:02:00.441 --> 00:02:04.963
"We may have 12 hours until Erik's explosion or only a few minutes.

29
00:02:05.923 --> 00:02:07.084
I have no idea."

30
00:02:07.104 --> 00:02:11.086
"Hush!" the Persian said.

31
00:02:11.106 --> 00:02:14.748
"I hear footsteps coming from Christine's room next door."

32
00:02:14.768 --> 00:02:17.009
"Raoul, Raoul!"

33
00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:18.390
Christine said.

34
00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.191
"Is that you I hear?

35
00:02:20.271 --> 00:02:21.372
Are you still alive?"

36
00:02:22.176 --> 00:02:23.737
"Yes, my darling."

37
00:02:23.757 --> 00:02:25.637
"Oh, thank goodness."

38
00:02:28.579 --> 00:02:30.800
Christine began to sob with relief.

39
00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.002
"I promised to marry Erik if he took me to the hexagon room, but he refused.

40
00:02:37.842 --> 00:02:42.004
He said there was no more need for that key and threw it in the lake.

41
00:02:42.824 --> 00:02:47.026
He's gone out for a few minutes to give me time to decide whether to marry him or

42
00:02:47.106 --> 00:02:50.347
not. Oh, Raoul, I thought you were dead.

43
00:02:50.367 --> 00:02:53.236
I thought—" "Mademoiselle!

44
00:02:53.516 --> 00:02:54.136
What time is it?"

45
00:02:54.637 --> 00:02:55.837
the Persian interrupted.

46
00:02:55.857 --> 00:02:57.918
"Five minutes before 11."

47
00:02:57.938 --> 00:03:00.618
"Yes, but in the morning or the evening?"

48
00:03:00.638 --> 00:03:02.159
"The evening.

49
00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:04.780
It's five minutes before I must make my decision.

50
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.441
Oh, Raoul, I'm so frightened.

51
00:03:08.041 --> 00:03:12.062
Erik tore off his mask, and his eyes shot yellow flames.

52
00:03:14.623 --> 00:03:20.825
Then he handed me the other key from his leather bag—the brass one—and said it opens both ebony

53
00:03:20.925 --> 00:03:22.567
boxes on my mantel.

54
00:03:23.387 --> 00:03:25.609
He called it the key of life or death.

55
00:03:26.309 --> 00:03:31.373
I don't understand why he said that, but—" "It must have something to do with the gunpowder," the

56
00:03:31.413 --> 00:03:32.213
Persian said.

57
00:03:32.233 --> 00:03:34.795
"Gunpowder?" Christine paused.

58
00:03:34.815 --> 00:03:37.417
"Erik didn't say anything about gunpowder.

59
00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:42.741
He said one box contained a brass scorpion and the other a brass grasshopper.

60
00:03:43.481 --> 00:03:47.224
If I turned the scorpion around, that meant I'd marry him.

61
00:03:47.980 --> 00:03:51.641
But if I turned the grasshopper, that meant my answer was no.

62
00:03:52.461 --> 00:03:55.922
His last words were 'Be careful of the grasshopper.

63
00:03:56.562 --> 00:03:57.263
It hops!

64
00:03:57.743 --> 00:03:59.303
It hops really high!'"

65
00:03:59.323 --> 00:04:02.724
"You know Erik," Raoul said to the Persian.

66
00:04:02.744 --> 00:04:06.665
"What do you think he means about the grasshopper hopping really high?"

67
00:04:06.685 --> 00:04:13.547
"I have no doubt that the grasshopper controls an electric current that will blow up the barrels of

68
00:04:13.607 --> 00:04:16.768
gunpowder." "Why do you keep talking about gunpowder?"

69
00:04:17.248 --> 00:04:18.229
Christine asked.

70
00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:25.213
Raoul quickly explained the situation to his fiancée and urged her to turn the scorpion at once.

71
00:04:25.233 --> 00:04:27.515
"No! Don't touch the scorpion!"

72
00:04:27.915 --> 00:04:28.816
the Persian cried.

73
00:04:28.836 --> 00:04:31.037
"What?" Raoul said.

74
00:04:31.057 --> 00:04:34.680
"This may be another trick of Erik's," the Persian said.

75
00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:40.023
"He couldn't really expect Christine to marry him, so perhaps he gave her the wrong information.

76
00:04:40.824 --> 00:04:45.387
Maybe he's hiding somewhere, waiting to watch the opera house explode from a safe distance."

77
00:04:46.247 --> 00:04:46.948
"Here he comes!"

78
00:04:47.404 --> 00:04:48.265
Christine said.

79
00:04:48.985 --> 00:04:53.608
The two men heard Erik's footsteps as he entered the room next door.

80
00:04:53.628 --> 00:04:55.550
"Hello, Erik!"

81
00:04:56.150 --> 00:04:58.011
the Persian called out boldly.

82
00:04:58.792 --> 00:05:05.856
With extraordinary calmness, Erik replied, "So you're not dead in there."

83
00:05:08.738 --> 00:05:12.281
The Persian tried to reply, but Erik silenced him.

84
00:05:12.301 --> 00:05:20.486
"Not a word or I shall blow up everything, and that honor belongs to Mademoiselle Christine.

85
00:05:21.587 --> 00:05:26.510
I see that she has not touched the scorpion or the grasshopper, but it's not too late to

86
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:27.571
do the right thing.

87
00:05:28.351 --> 00:05:30.433
Give me the key, my dear."

88
00:05:31.333 --> 00:05:35.816
Erik laughed wickedly and opened the two boxes for Christine.

89
00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.181
look at both creatures, shall we?

90
00:05:42.201 --> 00:05:44.242
Aren't they pretty?

91
00:05:45.283 --> 00:05:51.580
If you choose the grasshopper, we shall all be blown up because there is enough gunpowder under our

92
00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.841
feet to destroy the whole neighborhood.

93
00:05:54.542 --> 00:06:01.808
But if you choose the scorpion, the gunpowder will be soaked with water and made useless.

94
00:06:02.869 --> 00:06:11.236
All those Parisians upstairs listening to a second-rate opera will be forever grateful to you for saving their

95
00:06:11.316 --> 00:06:15.420
lives. And we shall be happily married!"

96
00:06:17.512 --> 00:06:18.353
Erik paused.

97
00:06:18.373 --> 00:06:25.757
"If you haven't turned the scorpion in two minutes, I will turn the grasshopper."

98
00:06:26.657 --> 00:06:34.522
There was a long, terrible silence during which Raoul prayed and the Persian listened to his heart pounding.

99
00:06:34.542 --> 00:06:38.084
"Your two minutes are up," Erik announced.

100
00:06:38.104 --> 00:06:40.365
"Good-bye, Christine.

101
00:06:41.226 --> 00:06:42.386
Hop, grasshopper!"

102
00:06:42.406 --> 00:06:45.128
"Wait!" Christine cried.

103
00:06:45.912 --> 00:06:49.094
"Do you promise that the scorpion is the right one to turn?"

104
00:06:49.114 --> 00:06:55.477
"Yes, but if you won't turn it now, I'll turn the grasshopper."

105
00:06:55.497 --> 00:06:57.799
"Erik!" "Enough!"

106
00:06:58.439 --> 00:06:59.260
Erik roared.

107
00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.861
"Erik! I've turned the scorpion!"

108
00:07:02.621 --> 00:07:08.665
The Persian and Raoul heard a hissing noise beneath their feet as water flowed into the room below

109
00:07:08.725 --> 00:07:15.012
them. They had forgotten their thirst, but now they ran to the bottom of the steps and drank

110
00:07:15.232 --> 00:07:15.772
and drank.

111
00:07:16.813 --> 00:07:21.334
As the water rose, they retreated up the staircase, step by step.

112
00:07:23.835 --> 00:07:28.136
The water came out of the cellar and flooded the floor of the hexagon room.

113
00:07:28.156 --> 00:07:30.476
"Erik, that's enough water.

114
00:07:31.157 --> 00:07:33.877
The gunpowder is soaked," the Persian called out.

115
00:07:33.897 --> 00:07:35.498
"Turn off the tap."

116
00:07:36.338 --> 00:07:39.139
"Christine, the water is up to our knees!"

117
00:07:39.639 --> 00:07:40.319
Raoul said.

118
00:07:41.179 --> 00:07:45.309
But Erik did not reply and neither did Christine.

119
00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:51.915
The Persian and Raoul were alone in the dark with the water rising and no one to turn

120
00:07:51.975 --> 00:07:52.575
off the tap.

121
00:07:53.296 --> 00:07:59.120
They lost their footing and were thrown against the mirrors as the water spun them around the hexagon

122
00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:01.762
room. "I love you, Christine!"

123
00:08:02.243 --> 00:08:03.044
Raoul called.

124
00:08:03.064 --> 00:08:06.386
"Erik, remember that I saved your life!"

125
00:08:06.847 --> 00:08:09.529
the Persian cried, but it was too late.

126
00:08:10.249 --> 00:08:15.014
The water filled the room, and the two men lost consciousness.
